{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_tmnycral7ljn767eqqdkutmpd5genmaohtauvbnqh4ndvpdg337a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass, and the work found and fixed a real translation bug.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md`: `nm -D` shows 0 missing symbols in Rust | ✅ `call_fma`, `driver`, `fma_array` — diff empty for both the harness and `--release` `.so` |\n| Phase B: every `CONFIGS.md` row passes over randomized inputs | ✅ 39/39 |\n| Phase C: every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing differential test | ✅ 27/27 (E1–E21, G1–G6), 25 test fns |\n| Holds under every feature combination | ✅ Cargo declares no `[features]` → 1 combination; verified across 6 build configs |\n\n## Phase A findings\n\nThe C library is one translation unit exporting 3 symbols, with **no** enums, flags, modes, structs, or global state, and no `#ifdef`/CMake options — so exactly one build configuration exists. All 3 C functions were already translated (no missing modules, no stubs).\n\n## The real bug (row E6)\n\n`call_fma` allocates three VLAs, `int out[len]; int ones[len]; int zeros[len];`. The translation used heap `Vec`s, which changes the failure mode:\n\n| `len` | C | Rust (before) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `1<<20` (12 MiB VLA) | `SIGSEGV` | survived allocation |\n| `INT_MAX` (25.7 GiB) | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` — allocator error |\n\nTwo divergences: a different fatal signal, and — for a caller passing a correctly sized buffer — Rust **returning a value where the C process dies**. Fixed in `src/lib.rs` with `probe_vla_stack`, which touches the same stack depth the C's VLAs consume before allocating, plus a fallible `try_reserve_exact` so Rust can never abort on an allocator error the C has no path for.\n\n## Two measurement bugs I had to fix first\n\nThese are worth flagging because either one silently fakes a green suite:\n\n1. **`cargo test` never builds the cdylib.** For a crate whose only `crate-type` is `cdylib`, `cargo test` builds an rlib and emits no `libdriver.so` — so loading `target/debug/libdriver.so` tests a *stale* artifact. My first `probe_vla_stack` fix appeared to do nothing for exactly this reason. The harness now compiles the `.so` under test itself with `rustc`.\n2. **In-process `dup2` capture swallowed libtest's progress text**, producing 6 \"mismatches\" whose two sides both contained the correct value. `driver` now runs in a forked child with a private pipe.\n\n## Two divergences that are *not* translation defects (documented, asserted per-config)\n\n- **`len < 0`** (E3/E4): C reads uninitialised memory past its own frame and returns different garbage on every run (`284418962`, `-1820306542`, `463860626`, …), or `SIGSEGV`. There is no value to be byte-identical to; Rust returns `0` deterministically, and the tests assert exact equality for the defined neighbours `len == 0` and `len == 1`.\n- **NULL deref under `debug_assertions`** (E5/E9): rustc's MIR null check turns it into an abort instead of `SIGSEGV`. Not suppressible from source, so the suite asserts the exact C signal in the shipping config and the Rust check's abort when assertions are on — both settings are exercised, not skipped. Relatedly, fault rows reset `SIGSEGV` to `SIG_DFL` in the child so the comparison sees the kernel's signal rather than Rust std's stack-overflow handler rewriting it.\n\n`sscanf` vs `__isoc99_sscanf` (the C and Rust `.so`s import different glibc entry points) is pinned by a dedicated 3000-case equivalence test rather than assumed benign. `c_src/` was not modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1080,"cache_read_input_tokens":203654,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1080,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b3045